{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '975,1380p'\"","aggregated_output":"   975\tThe common target is a small computation language with explicit language\n   976\truntime libraries.\n   977\t\n   978\tThe reference semantic ABI should define:\n   979\t\n   980\t- Evaluation order.\n   981\t- Argument binding and arity checks.\n   982\t- Language value encodings.\n   983\t- Identity and aliasing.\n   984\t- Scope and initialization states.\n   985\t- Normal and abrupt completion.\n   986\t- Effect requests and responses.\n   987\t- Suspend/resume behavior.\n   988\t\n   989\tDo not inherit Clojure truthiness, equality, integer arithmetic, or\n   990\targument binding merely because the frontend is implemented in Clojure.\n   991\t\n   992\tA practical baseline is an explicit state-passing and continuation-based\n   993\tlowering:\n   994\t\n   995\t- Immutable heap state is threaded through guest operations.\n   996\t- References are logical identities into that state.\n   997\t- Loops and control transfers become lambdas, applications, and\n   998\t  conditionals.\n   999\t- Abrupt completion is explicit, for example normal, return, throw,\n  1000\t  break, continue, or yield.\n  1001\t- Suspended state consists of portable code references and data.\n  1002\t\n  1003\tAliased guest objects share logical identity, not host mutable objects.\n  1004\tConcurrent access to a guest heap has one owning interpreter or an\n  1005\texplicitly defined stream protocol.\n  1006\t\n  1007\tGenerated names come from a deterministic, capture-avoiding name supply\n  1008\tin compiler state. Namespace-global `gensym` counters, request IDs,\n  1009\tsource positions, and host map iteration order must not affect canonical\n  1010\tcode.\n  1011\t\n  1012\tThe existing AST's `:vm/store-put` value is data, not an evaluated child.\n  1013\tDynamic assignment must use a supported effectful application or the\n  1014\texplicit state ABI; inserting an AST under `:val` would store syntax.\n  1015\t\n  1016\tRuntime values must also avoid accidental interpretation as engine\n  1017\teffects. Use a defined tagged value representation so a guest object\n  1018\tcontaining a property named `effect` cannot trigger `module/effect?`.\n  1019\t\n  1020\t### 8.2 Placement of semantics\n  1021\t\n  1022\t**Compiler desugaring** handles syntax-directed control, binding\n  1023\tanalysis, static checks, evaluation order, and construction of runtime\n  1024\toperations.\n  1025\t\n  1026\t**Portable prelude code** handles object systems, dispatch, coercions,\n  1027\tcollections, language exceptions, argument binding, and other\n  1028\tlanguage-visible behavior. Prefer Universal AST code so behavior is\n  1029\tinspectable and portable.\n  1030\t\n  1031\t**Profiled pure primitives** can accelerate expensive value operations\n  1032\twhen implementations have equivalent behavior across hosts.\n  1033\t\n  1034\t**Yin FFI/effects** handle actual host interaction: files, sockets,\n  1035\ttimers, processes, external libraries, and native resources. They are\n  1036\tstream request/response protocols with declared capabilities.\n  1037\t\n  1038\tCalling a host's native Python, JVM, or JavaScript evaluator is a\n  1039\tseparate compatibility service profile. It does not establish that the\n  1040\tprogram was compiled into portable Yin semantics.\n  1041\t\n  1042\t### 8.3 Paradigm summary\n  1043\t\n  1044\t```text\n  1045\t+------------------------+----------------------------+------------------+\n  1046\t| Paradigm               | Universal AST target       | Reference        |\n  1047\t|                        |                            | examples         |\n  1048\t+========================+============================+==================+\n  1049\t| Object-oriented,       | Records (runtime           | Java, PHP,       |\n  1050\t| class-based            | descriptors plus fields)   | Python classes   |\n  1051\t|                        | and method code; dispatch  |                  |\n  1052\t|                        | in prelude                 |                  |\n  1053\t+------------------------+----------------------------+------------------+\n  1054\t| Functional             | Direct lowering to lambda, | Clojure,         |\n  1055\t|                        | application, if, literal   | ClojureScript,   |\n  1056\t|                        |                            | ClojureDart,     |\n  1057\t|                        |                            | Haskell-style    |\n  1058\t+------------------------+----------------------------+------------------+\n  1059\t| Prototype / dynamic    | Closures plus store cells  | JavaScript       |\n  1060\t|                        | for mutable bindings and   |                  |\n  1061\t|                        | property maps              |                  |\n  1062\t+------------------------+----------------------------+------------------+\n  1063\t| Systems                | Explicit ownership state,  | Go, Rust, C      |\n  1064\t|                        | value copying, and effect  |                  |\n  1065\t|                        | descriptors for memory and |                  |\n  1066\t|                        | scheduling                 |                  |\n  1067\t+------------------------+----------------------------+------------------+\n  1068\t| (any further paradigm) | Added via the SPI; no core | non-exhaustive   |\n  1069\t|                        | change                     |                  |\n  1070\t+------------------------+----------------------------+------------------+\n  1071\t```\n  1072\t\n  1073\t### 8.4 Object-oriented: Java, static typing and class-based objects\n  1074\t\n  1075\tThe Java frontend performs declaration collection, resolution, type\n  1076\tchecking, overload selection, access checks, and relevant\n  1077\tdefinite-assignment checks before executable lowering.\n  1078\t\n  1079\tClasses become runtime descriptors plus explicit fields, method code,\n  1080\tconstructor logic, and initialization state. Interfaces contribute type\n  1081\tconstraints and dispatch contracts. Virtual calls use runtime dispatch;\n  1082\toverload resolution remains a compile-time responsibility. Evaluation\n  1083\torder must follow the selected Java edition (Java Language\n  1084\tSpecification, chapter 15).\n  1085\t\n  1086\tThe prelude must define:\n  1087\t\n  1088\t- Primitive numeric widths, overflow, conversions, and comparisons.\n  1089\t- Object identity, null behavior, arrays, and bounds failures.\n  1090\t- Inheritance, interface dispatch, and method invocation.\n  1091\t- Static members and class initialization lifecycle.\n  1092\t- Language exceptions and cleanup behavior.\n  1093\t\n  1094\tTyped variables do not require a new canonical node tag. Erased checking\n  1095\tinformation remains analysis data; runtime-observable type information\n  1096\tbecomes explicit program values.\n  1097\t\n  1098\tReflection, class loading, monitors, threads, and JNI require additional\n  1099\tsupport profiles. A class-based subset must not be labeled full JVM\n  1100\tcompatibility.\n  1101\t\n  1102\t### 8.5 Dynamic: Python, indentation and dynamic values\n  1103\t\n  1104\tPython is dynamically typed, but ordinary name resolution is based on\n  1105\tlexical scopes with explicit `global` and `nonlocal` rules. It is not\n  1106\tgenerally dynamically scoped. Whole-block binding analysis is needed to\n  1107\tdistinguish local variables and unbound-local errors (Python execution\n  1108\tmodel reference).\n  1109\t\n  1110\tThe frontend and prelude divide responsibilities as follows:\n  1111\t\n  1112\t- Indentation, line joining, and lexical state belong to the grammar\n  1113\t  package and its parsing helpers.\n  1114\t- Scope analysis precedes lowering.\n  1115\t- Assignments update explicit binding cells or threaded state; nested\n  1116\t  lambdas alone do not implement mutable captured variables.\n  1117\t- `and` and `or` preserve short-circuiting and return operand values.\n  1118\t- Comprehensions preserve evaluation order and their dialect-specific\n  1119\t  scope.\n  1120\t- Decorator expressions and application order are explicit; execution\n  1121\t  occurs when the definition is evaluated.\n  1122\t- Default arguments, keyword arguments, and variadic binding use\n  1123\t  Python's calling rules.\n  1124\t- Generators lower to explicit resumable state machines, including\n  1125\t  completion and injected exceptions.\n  1126\t\n  1127\tTruthiness, arbitrary-precision integers, division, equality, attribute\n  1128\tlookup, descriptors, and iteration belong to the Python runtime profile.\n  1129\t\n  1130\tGenerator support is incomplete until `send`, `throw`, `close`, cleanup,\n  1131\tand suspension are tested. It is not established by parsing `yield`.\n  1132\t\n  1133\t### 8.6 Object-oriented and web: PHP, mixed text and ordered maps\n  1134\t\n  1135\tThe lexer uses modes to preserve HTML/text and PHP regions in source\n  1136\torder. Text outside code lowers to ordered output effects; it is not\n  1137\tdiscarded by stripping tags.\n  1138\t\n  1139\tPHP arrays require ordered-map behavior, including key conversion and\n  1140\titeration rules. A plain host hash map is not a sufficient\n  1141\trepresentation.\n  1142\t\n  1143\tThe semantic profile must cover:\n  1144\t\n  1145\t- `$var` binding and function/global/static scope.\n  1146\t- Capture by value versus capture by reference.\n  1147\t- References and aliasing.\n  1148\t- Ordered arrays and observable copy behavior.\n  1149\t- Coercion, comparisons, missing values, and error behavior.\n  1150\t- Function and class calls, including supported type declarations.\n  1151\t\n  1152\tSuperglobals are explicit request-context values supplied by the\n  1153\tcomposition. They do not read ambient process or HTTP state.\n  1154\t\n  1155\t`include` and `require` perform module-resolution effects with recorded\n  1156\tinputs. `eval` is a new compilation request, subject to the same\n  1157\tprofiles and diagnostics.\n  1158\t\n  1159\tThe existing frontend's ignored type hints and limited closure handling\n  1160\tare migration limitations, not semantics to preserve as the new default.\n  1161\t\n  1162\t### 8.7 Prototype: JavaScript, lexical environments and jobs\n  1163\t\n  1164\tThe frontend distinguishes script and module goals and pins an\n  1165\tECMAScript edition.\n  1166\t\n  1167\tIt lowers:\n  1168\t\n  1169\t- `var` bindings and initialization.\n  1170\t- `let` and `const`, block environments, and temporal dead zones.\n  1171\t- Per-iteration bindings where required.\n  1172\t- Ordinary functions versus arrow functions, including `this` behavior.\n  1173\t- Property access, receiver binding, and prototype lookup.\n  1174\t- Short-circuiting and completion propagation.\n  1175\t\n  1176\tThe prelude defines property descriptors, prototype chains, coercion,\n  1177\tequality, `undefined`, numeric behavior, and other supported object\n  1178\toperations. ECMAScript environment records and execution contexts supply\n  1179\tthe semantic reference.\n  1180\t\n  1181\tPromises and `async`/`await` require an explicit job interpreter. A\n  1182\tlanguage-level callback is represented as guest callable data scheduled\n  1183\tby that interpreter. It is never installed directly as a host callback.\n  1184\t\n  1185\tObservable job ordering, rejection propagation, and suspension must be\n  1186\ttested separately from successful value computation. Browser APIs and\n  1187\tNode APIs are effect packages, not properties of the JavaScript grammar.\n  1188\t\n  1189\t### 8.8 Functional: Clojure and its hosts\n  1190\t\n  1191\tThe Clojure frontend already exists (`yang.clojure`) and lowers directly:\n  1192\tforms become `:lambda`, `:application`, `:if`, `:literal`, and\n  1193\t`:variable` nodes, and definitions become explicit `yin/def`\n  1194\tapplications. It implements the section 3 contract through its existing\n  1195\treader rather than through ANTLR. ClojureScript and ClojureDart are\n  1196\tdialects of the same frontend distinguished by dialect options and\n  1197\truntime profile, not by separate core branches.\n  1198\t\n  1199\t### 8.9 Systems: Go, Rust, C, and further extensions\n  1200\t\n  1201\tFuture system-language frontends reuse the SPI but contribute additional\n  1202\tsemantic analysis and runtime profiles:\n  1203\t\n  1204\t- Structures and value copying.\n  1205\t- Logical references, explicit memory regions, and layouts where\n  1206\t  observable.\n  1207\t- Pattern matching and exhaustiveness checks.\n  1208\t- Ownership, borrowing, moves, and destruction where applicable.\n  1209\t- Defined panic/unwind or error behavior.\n  1210\t\n  1211\tGo's value and reference-bearing types need their specified copying\n  1212\tbehavior; goroutines and channels require a language scheduler above\n  1213\tstreams rather than treating a broadcast log as a destructive channel.\n  1214\t\n  1215\tRust requires ownership and lifetime analysis beyond parsing, plus\n  1216\texplicit drop behavior. Those analyses may be an additional service\n  1217\tstage without changing the frontend SPI.\n  1218\t\n  1219\tRaw native pointers are host capabilities, never portable literals.\n  1220\tUnsafe/native operations need a separately declared profile.\n  1221\t\n  1222\tC preprocessing, Rust macro expansion, SQL dialect resolution, TypeScript\n  1223\tchecking, and Ruby dynamic dispatch are plugin-owned stages. Each has\n  1224\texplicit inputs, outputs, and dependencies. None requires Yang core to\n  1225\trecognize its language by name.\n  1226\t\n  1227\t### 8.10 Cross-language calls\n  1228\t\n  1229\tA common AST does not imply a common object model.\n  1230\t\n  1231\tInterop exports need an explicit ABI describing scalar conversion,\n  1232\tstrings, numeric ranges, callable arguments, exceptions, object handles,\n  1233\townership, and asynchronous results.\n  1234\t\n  1235\tStart with a small portable-value interop profile. Rich object sharing\n  1236\trequires adapters; JavaScript `null`, Python `None`, PHP `null`, and Java\n  1237\tnull references must not be conflated accidentally.\n  1238\t\n  1239\t---\n  1240\t\n  1241\t## 9. Standard library and FFI architecture\n  1242\t\n  1243\tEvery language arrives with a standard library, and most of what a\n  1244\tprogram observes as \"the language\" is that library. This section places\n  1245\teach part of a language's standard library and host interface in one of\n  1246\tfour layers. The placement follows the semantic placement rule of\n  1247\tsection 8.2 and the host-boundary rule of `datom.world.md`: portable\n  1248\tcode appends effects, host interpreters perform them and append outcomes,\n  1249\tand no host function is ever called from portable code.\n  1250\t\n  1251\t### 9.1 The four-layer model\n  1252\t\n  1253\t```text\n  1254\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  1255\t| Layer | Contents                    | How it reaches the VM         |\n  1256\t+=======+=============================+===============================+\n  1257\t| 1     | Pure modules: library code  | Compiled by Yang, exactly as  |\n  1258\t|       | written in the language     | user code; no runtime         |\n  1259\t|       | itself                      | special-casing                |\n  1260\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  1261\t| 2     | Language intrinsics and     | Universal AST prelude per     |\n  1262\t|       | preludes: the initial       | language, compiled once,      |\n  1263\t|       | environment                 | content-addressed, fetched    |\n  1264\t|       |                             | from the dao.jing DHT         |\n  1265\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  1266\t| 3     | Object system desugaring:   | Records, store cells, and     |\n  1267\t|       | classes, prototypes, traits | closures; no object concept   |\n  1268\t|       |                             | enters the VM kernel          |\n  1269\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  1270\t| 4     | System I/O and effects:     | Yin VM effect descriptors;    |\n  1271\t|       | files, sockets, timers,     | Local dao.stream.apply FFI;   |\n  1272\t|       | processes, native libraries | host interpreters append      |\n  1273\t|       |                             | outcomes                      |\n  1274\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  1275\t```\n  1276\t\n  1277\t### 9.2 Layer 1: pure modules\n  1278\t\n  1279\tSource in the language itself, compiled to Universal AST via Yang\n  1280\tdirectly. A Python `itertools` written in Python, a Java collections\n  1281\tclass written in Java, a Clojure `clojure.core` function written in\n  1282\tClojure: each is an ordinary compilation unit that passes through the\n  1283\tsection 5 ingestion and section 6 pipeline and is admitted like any\n  1284\tother program. There is no runtime special-casing: the evaluator cannot\n  1285\ttell library code from user code, and nothing in Yang core names a\n  1286\tlibrary module.\n  1287\t\n  1288\t### 9.3 Layer 2: language intrinsics and preludes\n  1289\t\n  1290\tA Universal AST prelude per language seeds the initial environment. It\n  1291\tcarries what section 8.2 assigns to portable prelude code: value\n  1292\tencodings, truthiness, equality, arithmetic semantics, argument binding,\n  1293\tcoercions, collections, and language exceptions.\n  1294\t\n  1295\tA prelude is compiled once and content-addressed; its root addresses are\n  1296\tnamed in the frontend's runtime profile (section 3.1, part 4). It is\n  1297\tdistributed via the `dao.jing` DHT, so every host that runs a language\n  1298\tresolves the same prelude by the same address and never rebuilds it. A\n  1299\tcomposition that pins a runtime profile pins its prelude addresses; two\n  1300\tcompositions running different prelude revisions do not interfere.\n  1301\t\n  1302\tPreludes are Universal AST code, so they are inspectable, queryable by\n  1303\t`dao.space.query/q` over their rows, and portable across CLJ, CLJS, and\n  1304\tCLJD without per-host reimplementation.\n  1305\t\n  1306\t### 9.4 Layer 3: object system desugaring\n  1307\t\n  1308\tClass, prototype, and trait hierarchies are desugared to records, store\n  1309\tcells, and closures:\n  1310\t\n  1311\t- A class becomes a runtime descriptor record plus field layout and\n  1312\t  method code (section 8.4).\n  1313\t- A prototype chain becomes property maps in store cells linked by\n  1314\t  logical identity (section 8.7).\n  1315\t- A trait or interface becomes a dispatch contract the prelude resolves\n  1316\t  at call time.\n  1317\t\n  1318\tNo object system concept crosses into the VM kernel. The canonical\n  1319\tgrammar of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 2.3 has no class, method,\n  1320\tprototype, or trait tag, and this document adds none. Dispatch, method\n  1321\tresolution, and inheritance are prelude code (layer 2) operating on data\n  1322\tthat desugaring produced.\n  1323\t\n  1324\t### 9.5 Layer 4: system I/O and effects\n  1325\t\n  1326\tSystem I/O is expressed as Yin VM effect descriptors. Portable code\n  1327\tappends a descriptor naming the operation, its arguments, and a\n  1328\tcorrelation identity; a host interpreter consumes that stream, performs\n  1329\tthe operation, and appends the outcome; portable code reads the outcome.\n  1330\tA host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified\n  1331\tunsupported result, which is a correct outcome.\n  1332\t\n  1333\tFFI calls use `dao.stream.apply` as the VM's local FFI bridge. The call\n  1334\tis a request record on a stream, the result is a response record, and\n  1335\tcorrelation is by identity in the data. The apply wire envelope is\n  1336\tretired from the network path; remote service traffic uses request and\n  1337\tanswer streams over `dao.stream.remote` reflections. No direct host\n  1338\tfunction calls from portable code. Browser APIs, Node APIs, JVM\n  1339\tlibraries, and Dart platform channels are effect packages declared in\n  1340\ta frontend's runtime profile, not properties of any grammar.\n  1341\t\n  1342\tCapabilities are declared: the runtime profile lists the effects a\n  1343\tlanguage's standard library may request, and a composition grants or\n  1344\twithholds them.\n  1345\t\n  1346\t### 9.6 Module distribution\n  1347\t\n  1348\tStandard-library modules, preludes, and any compiled package are\n  1349\tcontent-addressed packages in the `dao.jing` DHT. A package is a manifest\n  1350\tnaming the root addresses it exports plus the rows those roots reach;\n  1351\tequal content is equal address across hosts. Module resolution consumes\n  1352\trequests and emits pinned module artifacts (section 11); a dependency\n  1353\tedge names a content address, never a search path or a network location.\n  1354\t\n  1355\t---\n  1356\t\n  1357\t## 10. Diagnostics and error reporting\n  1358\t\n  1359\tANTLR's default behavior combines error reporting and recovery. Console\n  1360\treporting is supplied by a listener, while recovery may insert/delete\n  1361\ttokens, continue, or raise recognition errors. The integration must\n  1362\tcontrol both mechanisms.\n  1363\t\n  1364\t### 10.1 Boundary policy\n  1365\t\n  1366\tFor lexer and parser:\n  1367\t\n  1368\t1. Remove default console listeners.\n  1369\t2. Install boundary transformations that classify diagnostics as plain\n  1370\t   data.\n  1371\t3. Select a pinned recovery strategy.\n  1372\t4. Catch recognized parsing failures at the host boundary.\n  1373\t5. Export recovery markers and terminal parse status.\n  1374\t6. Retain no exception, recognizer, token, or host stack object in\n  1375\t   payloads.\n  1376\t\n  1377\tA default strict profile refuses executable publication after any\n  1378\tlexical or syntax error, even when ANTLR constructed a recovered CST. An\n  1379\tIDE profile may publish partial syntax for tooling on a separate medium.\n  1380\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
