{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-30 14:03:08 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 21:03:08 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n| D# | decision | rationale | risk | confidence (high/med/low) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 | **Confirm copy-on-lift**, preserving aliases and cycles within the entire transferred value graph. Slice 1 refuses cell-bearing lifts. | Each receiving task owns an independent heap snapshot; sibling closures transferred together must retain their shared cell. This fits task ownership without cross-task mutation. Copying is a chosen transport contract, however, not the only possible consequence of the invariants. | Separate transfers produce separate identities. Repeated module linking must not split aliases between existing module stores and newly lowered exports. | high |\n| D2 | **Use `cell/new`, `cell/get`, `cell/set!`; state `:heap`, portable section `:yin.k/heap`.** | Binding cells describe the intended language semantics accurately. Explicitly distinguish heap cells from cursor cells; the existing cursor encoding need not change. | Implementers may accidentally reuse cursor-specific machinery or conflate the two identity spaces. | high |\n| D3 | **Amend §8.1 to an explicit task-owned cell heap**, updated through persistent VM-state transitions. | The heap remains immutable data at each transition; guest code no longer threads it through every function argument. Align §8.5 and §9.4 terminology, preserve logical identity, and state the initial portability limitation. | Describing this merely as “mutable heap” could invite host mutation or imply migration support that slice 1 lacks. | high |\n| D4 | **Fix before the executable spike, as a separate prerequisite to cell slice 1. Use callee-profile-authorized effect dispatch.** | Retain trusted callable profiles at registration/installation and build a task-local callable-identity lookup, avoiding per-call registry scans. Pure results always remain values. Effectful constructors must return a valid descriptor whose kind belongs to their declared effect set. Apply this uniformly across four kernels, including higher-order calls and module exports. | Existing code may depend on accidental shape dispatch. Reject conflicting profiles for the same callable identity; unknown callables must not gain effect authority from their results. | high |\n| D5 | **Accept cells and continuation lowering; strengthen the spike rule to box every function-local binding and parameter.** | “Every reassigned local” is insufficient without binding analysis: first assignment after capture, forward closure capture, and recursive local functions can also require shared locations. Allocate cells on function entry, initialize parameters, and use an explicit unbound sentinel for other locals. Keep control transfers as continuations. | More allocation and heap growth. Boxing alone does not implement `finally`, exception targeting, or cleanup on abrupt exits. | high |\n| D6 | **Require qualified defects for malformed in-machine continuations before the spike.** | Invalid authenticity, kernel identity, and control structure must fail before restoration, with consistent diagnostic categories across kernels. Validation must permit legitimate live closures and primitives; external plain-data gates cannot simply be reused. | Overly restrictive validation could reject valid captures; broad exception-catching could conceal engine bugs. | high |\n| D7 | **Seal continuations now, before the spike, using sealed references to private captured payloads.** | A guest-editable map must not supply authoritative control fields. Store each captured payload in explicit task state, issue a sealed reference, authenticate it, then restore the private payload. A seal over kind and ID alone is insufficient if invocation still trusts payload fields supplied alongside it. | Adds retained continuation state and reclamation work. This closes continuation forgery, not the separate issue of forgeable closure-shaped maps. | med |\n| D8 | **Approve retirement, but do not remove the worktree yet. Preserve its differing gate prompt first; then remove worktree and merged branch.** | Both worktrees point to `8f9f90b0`, and the branch is merged. However, byte comparison contradicts “collab synced”: two untracked files match, while `1790769087413-reviewer-continuation-invocation-gate.prompt.md` does not match the main-worktree copy. Preserve that variant before cleanup. | Immediate forced removal would discard unique task context. | high |\n| D9 | **Untrack the 11 files going forward; preserve local copies and history.** | Use a scoped `git rm --cached` for the verified tracked files and add an appropriate ignore rule. Durable architectural decisions should live in design documents. History rewriting is unnecessary for ordinary collaboration artifacts. | Untracking without ignoring permits accidental re-addition; removing artifacts before transferring authoritative decisions weakens discoverability. | high |\n| D10 | **D4 → D6/D7 → cell slice 1 → JVM-only Python ANTLR execution spike.** | All three safeguards precede guest execution. Slice 1 includes sealed cells, fresh task heaps, explicit lift/completion refusal, documentation, and four-kernel parity checks. Then use pinned grammars-v4 Python3 sources, a generic ParseTree-to-data walker, scope analysis, case-per-rule lowering to Universal AST, continuations, and the strengthened boxing rule. Parser-only exploration may proceed earlier. | Grammar coverage can exceed semantic coverage. Publish a bounded subset; reject unsupported rules explicitly. Defer generators, heap migration, and full Python compatibility. | high |\n\nThese are **Astra’s independent Round 1 decisions**, not a claim of two-seat consensus. No files were edited; no tests were run.\n\nObjections to the cell ruling:\n\n1. **The minimal boxing rule is too weak.** Python whole-block scope analysis is already required by [yang.antlr.md:1104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1104). Captures retain an environment at [ast_walker.cljc:530](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:530), and invocation restores it at [ast_walker.cljc:261](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:261). Consider `def f(): return x; x = 1; return f()` inside an enclosing function: `x` has only one assignment, but `f` needs the location established before that assignment. Box all local bindings for the spike; optimize only after binding and liveness analysis.\n\n2. **Cells are a sound baseline, not a proof that alternatives are impossible.** [§8.1:992](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:992) explicitly permits state passing, and [§8.10:1229](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1229) already requires an interop ABI. Those approaches have costs, but the ruling overstates their impossibility and inevitable allocation behavior. Likewise, a generator needs evolving identity, not necessarily this particular cell primitive. Choose cells because they simplify the selected lowering.\n\n3. **Continuation authenticity must protect the authoritative payload.** Current recognition checks only the tag at [engine.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:60). Existing resource seals cover only secret, kind, and ID at [engine.cljc:261](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:261). Copying that seal onto a continuation map while trusting its editable `:k` or `:env` would leave tampering possible. A private payload table gives the seal a stable, authoritative referent without hashing live host functions.\n\n4. **The cursor lift is not a cycle-safe implementation template.** [engine.cljc:606](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:606) constructs the cursor payload before recording its mapping. Recursive heap encoding must instead reserve the identity **before traversing contents**, and lowering must allocate identities before filling cells. Also, the repeated-link issue at [engine.cljc:847](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:847) is a correctness gate for slice 2, not merely a wart.\n\n5. **F1 requires preserving authority that current registration discards.** Host registration stores raw functions and profile addresses at [module.cljc:210](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:210); application then passes only the primitive’s result onward at [ast_walker.cljc:251](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:251). Replacing `effect?` alone cannot fix this. Carry trusted profile authority through callable resolution and invocation, and validate the declared effect kind."}}
